#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290} :activity/id #uuid "55c2127a-d43f-53c5-907e-c8d12316517c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158562 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X,$ respectively. \n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ the expected value of the the random variable $X^2,$ we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6591 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3dc1ae12-0989-5596-8114-7d74427919d2" :step/math-academy-id 39753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156994 :question/id #uuid "65957407-b302-51c5-93d9-0f71f3517362" :question/math-academy-id "e-10527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508093}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "897313ee-485e-51e5-8fec-b665d482b874" :step/math-academy-id 39754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156995 :question/id #uuid "292610ec-9c27-590d-b207-c76adc959d55" :question/math-academy-id "e-10528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511527}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "08325c1e-fe0c-5a7b-b6e5-8f27c2951461" :step/math-academy-id 39755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156996 :question/id #uuid "4c5666a4-f477-5b1a-8d17-1e2aa594863f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "bce87273-1a01-58a6-825f-736ef62ef788" :step/math-academy-id 39756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158563 :tutorial/content "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X.$\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." :tutorial/id #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6592 :tutorial/title "Higher Moments"} :step/id #uuid "9fb3611b-a9ba-58f4-9159-00a7c9b29393" :step/math-academy-id 39757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158293}}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157533}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479151} :activity/id #uuid "f961c2fd-b375-5e2b-8cfd-ec028dc7aa0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e8f87d-a86b-5e5a-9b4c-520af1d67778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5de172-0879-5846-8775-50301538400b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478775} {:answer/id #uuid "8c404e33-978f-5492-8afd-92388f08864c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478781} {:answer/id #uuid "92a6058f-3718-55b4-83a2-afc873b42494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478797} {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b35dd5-9730-5404-b881-f84b63c0e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac78b028-e0aa-5c92-b54a-b1ba1527a82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152915" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "121da9a3-a27d-57e3-b7b6-52d9b4081634" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cb4776-7d73-5e89-be66-3abb80c22cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427291} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5bf664-a18f-566d-a4f7-3bd738b55f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427301} {:answer/id #uuid "74d74ac0-e401-549d-95e1-5b551db31568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427309} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2240d8-61b4-5dec-86e4-1ecedc83ab1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427322} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1156f5-fddd-5d6b-adf1-9732ee27312f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427333}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427291} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e8ebaf-8a75-5cb6-8621-5bcf5f7ac9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63bcffd8-dbf0-5c48-8ca6-56531c05d1c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131141" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,{e}^{-2}<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}},$ compute $\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X]=4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,|}_{{e}^{-2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X] & =4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}})-2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ec8cc12-b2a4-5daf-96fd-1e378052ed2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cfd005b-75a8-5b0f-ba56-4a0ed93ab33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478604} {:answer/id #uuid "22afcee8-e19d-562c-b205-332bc6a88919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478613} {:answer/id #uuid "7a90c263-2858-513a-872f-0ae63784d35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478741} {:answer/id #uuid "edf857b5-69b2-5c68-8336-4b0f3c36ec60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478931} {:answer/id #uuid "ff85e4b7-ab02-503c-8793-d2b775c75049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478958} :answer-field/id #uuid "623ea09b-af47-50f2-9745-0713c7359869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6cddda8-c777-5283-b03a-3671533a782a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131153" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,1<x<2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}]=10-4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=2$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[10-4{X}^{2}] & =10-4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =10-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "46c499f6-0d50-5bd9-9f52-26ed8cb8ebb6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a69b927d-fd51-516f-aae0-cf0da09a7720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153881} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ba8dfc-c937-598b-a9a5-dc59f7c01e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153882} {:answer/id #uuid "eb402453-8e9e-5d3c-a06e-96fbfc84d9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153883} {:answer/id #uuid "f418ec1e-ed9d-5bce-a47f-762f1c808098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153884} {:answer/id #uuid "63a0df93-12b0-5a94-8153-cab57c777d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153883} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e9af8f-c53a-563f-b1d5-c1fb676708fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d40b16f4-c022-5679-aaee-aa1be46926d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149708" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5},\\,0<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20},$ compute $\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]=\\frac{3}{10}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{20}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "473a0974-6e51-5843-94d7-0cf17d7cd776" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1370ffbd-2eab-5260-a0b4-0bf5d3cdb6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478582} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3f6cb5-53fa-5af5-81d3-830801107641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478750} {:answer/id #uuid "82d2a8f9-d415-5d71-a7d9-fe699cab072f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478758} {:answer/id #uuid "bedb09af-8e03-5cb2-a39f-8ac2f91ce3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} {:answer/id #uuid "e89460a9-557b-5055-9a71-6a052e322419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} :answer-field/id #uuid "aded51f8-58f0-5888-884c-244971621142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2317ff10-80a3-5747-858a-863cca47fbc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152922" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{x}){|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(1-0)+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0bf133b-cce4-5d5c-905a-ed7bcb9e9bdc"}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478561}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508148} :activity/id #uuid "3d4cf227-5451-5675-a3d0-310e1e202b0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747 :topic/id #uuid "bac6604a-5262-5144-8ceb-19f17d508317" :topic/math-academy-id 2987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8146a1-89ab-5805-b0d1-3cb41fad7d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} {:answer/id #uuid "77b5e97e-bc6c-5d70-b0ab-61c62f43eefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434406} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c4a89b-c4d2-5db7-98e5-df1b9a18f200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434414} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd85839-7391-5b9a-80bf-f734af24df0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434415} {:answer/id #uuid "c0834f80-0b5a-5af1-a67a-b29a8e35f4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} :answer-field/id #uuid "29094946-ea57-508c-a265-54b76fd27fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a18a122-3ad0-51db-8855-5d861bdec3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152917" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{8}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0aef0e95-7b36-56cc-a917-a6909d024646"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e8f87d-a86b-5e5a-9b4c-520af1d67778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5de172-0879-5846-8775-50301538400b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478775} {:answer/id #uuid "8c404e33-978f-5492-8afd-92388f08864c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478781} {:answer/id #uuid "92a6058f-3718-55b4-83a2-afc873b42494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478797} {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b35dd5-9730-5404-b881-f84b63c0e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac78b028-e0aa-5c92-b54a-b1ba1527a82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152915" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62dbfd8d-d6c8-5022-be2b-1ea1fd60fadf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2368f6-3f68-54b0-9d32-a27cbf3895be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507648} {:answer/id #uuid "b02c68e4-e61b-5654-9879-70de70a8fe6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507843} {:answer/id #uuid "b269375e-588f-5e5f-9ae9-591c8a3221c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507845} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd68903-0ef9-5e2a-88ed-3677ac3ae07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507864} {:answer/id #uuid "c01cb739-f7ab-5ebb-8e34-daf477537efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-5e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507864} :answer-field/id #uuid "440eb13c-81c4-5c5d-9faa-611d1e590780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8f3b4b6-eb37-510a-a5f6-48850ac9d684" :question/math-academy-id "q-131140" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,1<x<e\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=e-1,$ compute $\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X]=2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=e-1$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X] & =2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-(e-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1-e+1 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "81573813-f419-5bab-b167-8255dfe1a85b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507622} {:answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} {:answer/id #uuid "67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507751} {:answer/id #uuid "7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507784} {:answer/id #uuid "8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :question/math-academy-id "q-90392" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}},\\,x>1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a28c654f-55ef-5916-869c-70e534152c19" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b58a9e1-f2e5-5bab-86c3-1dc62a5bf1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434407} {:answer/id #uuid "83994b5b-dec3-525b-9473-71abebd50a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} {:answer/id #uuid "908f1924-a4cc-5cae-83ec-6a4c7872904f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434410} {:answer/id #uuid "93d5d0a4-79ad-5a4d-854e-315f284a4eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434411} {:answer/id #uuid "a25f9bac-857f-5e6a-8fee-76b7ccf2820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9c34c2-a686-5dff-8116-1e613117e2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf5c8a43-29b3-589c-9d23-ef6cc2a81919" :question/math-academy-id "q-131142" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,1<x<3\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ compute $\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}]=5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\,|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=2$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{13}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}] & =5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =10-13 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b234828c-5807-5de4-a2a5-5f231ccf4ede" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508137}}] :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507600}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125311} :activity/id #uuid "2bfe5aae-e1d5-50d4-96df-8e775e21203d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125741 :tutorial/content "The partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $f(x,y) = \\ln(xy).$ We wish to determine the set of values $(x,y)$ on which the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists.\n\nFirst, we need to find the domain of $f$ since we cannot differentiate $f$ at any point that lies outside of its domain. The natural logarithm is defined only for positive inputs, so the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy > 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ (also denoted $f_x$) is not defined at $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_x})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy \\gt 0 \\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5862db65-6f2d-5256-968d-cb971dd6b2f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2532 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e4bac5fb-b77d-59b1-8380-2ff0cf754d5c" :step/math-academy-id 11425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124269 :question/id #uuid "a663f68e-1fb9-5653-a128-1b55c2a58769" :question/math-academy-id "e-3449"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436750}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists"} :step/id #uuid "d81b9ea9-a347-5ebb-91d1-4bc5c4b566e0" :step/math-academy-id 11426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124270 :question/id #uuid "7297f213-2db2-5da2-9bba-cac7e31f6b64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493937}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"} :step/id #uuid "f4cc9d43-0b3e-5cd2-8e04-c1f0f43e9e2a" :step/math-academy-id 11429 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125742 :tutorial/content "From single-variable calculus, if a function $y=f(x)$ is differentiable at a point, then it is continuous at that point.\n\nBut for a multivariable function, even if all partial derivatives are defined at a certain point, it **does not mean** that the function is continuous at that point!\n\nThe reason why partial differentiability does not imply continuity in the case of a multivariable function is that\n\n- the existence of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ depend only on the behavior of $f$ parallel to the $x$- and $y$-axes, whereas\n\n- the continuity of $f$ depends on its behavior as we approach $(x_0,y_0)$ from any direction.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multivariable function:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5aad599789d1e317ff3ddc07eaa418e80469307f67e98e2108660fc7d15701.svg)\n\nThe function $f$ is defined at the point $(0,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(0,0)=0\n$$\n\nLikewise, the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ are both defined at the point $(0,0).$ We can compute them using the limit definition of the partial derivative, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0+h,0)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(h)(0)}{{h}^{2}+{0}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0,0+h)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(0)(h)}{{0}^{2}+{h}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the partial derivatives both exist at $(0,0).$ We will now show that the function is not continuous at $(0,0).$\n\nFor the function $f$ to be continuous at $(0,0),$ the limit of the function must be equal to the function value, regardless of the path taken. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{(x,y) \\to (0,0)}  f(x,y) = f(0,0) =0.\n$$\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. When we evaluate the limit along the path $y=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (0,0)\\,\\,y=x}f(x,y) & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{x\\cdot x}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f(x,y)$ is *not* continuous at the point $(0,0),$ even though both of its partial derivatives exist there." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca47070b-a1d3-53ad-8f57-2dbd4e770dce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2533 :tutorial/title "Partial Differentiability Does Not Imply Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "f23e88d8-c204-5795-b172-b05000a202c3" :step/math-academy-id 11432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124366 :question/id #uuid "97192aac-6c1a-5ead-962b-0bf7ab573661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436751}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"} :step/id #uuid "c86dfe84-cedf-55cf-bf44-efb12a49ac66" :step/math-academy-id 22675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124725}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409562} :activity/id #uuid "59747c22-b269-5844-bdda-f1750192841c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "899f43e4-fc9a-5506-b84d-a25c2d9eb6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453928} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4690b4-12f7-5ffd-a0f0-d137fa95703c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453928} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ccf0ae7-dc64-51f0-892e-8d88c6910e82" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376cf739-f8d8-5cb0-91a0-52b41ea250c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453930} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcc07c3-7704-591c-aebf-ff0249e9dd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453931} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f29120-e8c2-52fc-abd7-8386d5f878c4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc77c615-3280-58d4-a19d-18282a3f7486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453932} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfc9ce4-4706-5c6b-8f71-059b43d404d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453932} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c89136-ff34-5898-9f58-5a98f17bcfb1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72010" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II  only'."} :step/id #uuid "06a4b700-aa67-595f-ad77-c3a9c2d08fa3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b4579a6-884f-5620-9c48-03f859207fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337292} {:answer/id #uuid "aa211335-2ec0-5533-aaf0-0747d0111e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337566} {:answer/id #uuid "ab12ccaa-59a3-5391-8885-0e57cfa9220d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337575} {:answer/id #uuid "d30b5af1-be96-52a2-983e-53d94d787930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337875} {:answer/id #uuid "d932ef34-432a-58eb-96dc-98456b7b3465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337292} :answer-field/id #uuid "167ce942-9903-5739-b46e-634adbd1db2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "329fb25a-c39e-587a-8c51-720f867dc9e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72005" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1786d23c-6b2b-5b25-8a34-a3df54af58bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c4f7c1-2cc1-57ed-a18b-bf91992e00d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451483} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e4c2de-8568-5ff2-baf8-40d15c25b5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451484} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c60c75-d597-5852-8b29-5420ba46e80b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e8d67b-c006-521e-ad4e-1b3b5e5bbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451485} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcff0a4-c6d8-5ff7-9344-ecba3634e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451485} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8a887b-1bb0-5b36-af98-54f46b4ff833" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd09d87-8f09-5958-88e2-49043d0205b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451487} {:answer/id #uuid "72089ef2-d948-531c-b1f3-ac84e9dc1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451488} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4fe7e1-7135-5cca-a3a0-20d39be1b879" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b22bd39f-49d5-56c7-8797-705e83876e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-65255" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "488b6ab6-cae5-5979-9b52-c3723c3f093b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1625c29f-0f6c-51a9-9abf-da854c690be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0,\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445247} {:answer/id #uuid "603bfd17-5e73-595d-9e4e-67eb66547b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5cf9f7-9ed5-55ea-b3dd-342a00251b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445253} {:answer/id #uuid "b33e9175-1fe3-525c-84d8-993f2fb37971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445255} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f2980b-11d7-556d-9928-b285bb77ebda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253" :question/math-academy-id "q-132654" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61817e36-5704-54ae-b054-a7bf550b56c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e148fac7-727b-55d9-ba67-0a9fc7a276e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453368} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6ec723-5cd2-5173-b8f1-1778f583295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453369} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8e1392-18d1-56b3-b0f5-ff3dd861687a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a536093-cb71-5d44-a52f-8e05b4b9f74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453370} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2d5ab2-a228-5241-aef5-decbffea282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453371} :answer-field/id #uuid "deacc4af-75f1-5a94-a0d0-5d1ad4ac3ea8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b4f556-0cc0-5fc7-9fac-56ff9260d6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453372} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd91732-9818-5742-9c5d-ea668d6dbe0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7af479-38a3-58de-bca6-c137a88e2790" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a3b6388-98bd-5083-8d1c-f60a05890b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-132726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(\\sqrt{3},1)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3},1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}_{y}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is also true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{1})=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,1)=-\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "be87cbb6-8a21-5895-a193-08d9d1a311c5"}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409538}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426724} :activity/id #uuid "5947b5e4-3c04-5937-9aec-95b10c3ffed0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53636330-9403-5db0-9d17-5dda7fa6783b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451561} {:answer/id #uuid "845ef4ab-3d2a-5524-947f-6cbe1fb7da82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451562}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451561} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f9e12b-29cf-59f8-bf91-cc9a2f919c92" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b41f94-5879-57a9-a336-092f8f1aede9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451563} {:answer/id #uuid "d65c989f-2d09-5160-b064-ed15ba040057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451564} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fa8ff5f-ebba-5ee9-a165-1c4d49347c97" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5f6a07-7680-53e1-ae55-7331a6a7e9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451565} {:answer/id #uuid "c818b116-0775-5d48-9343-c6660016175b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451566}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451566} :answer-field/id #uuid "45930e95-82c6-577a-8b28-a081c55cca47" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ada5bc24-8a74-58e6-b57b-4b21dfa8df4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72022" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy(z-1))?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The partial derivative with respect to $z$ is defined at $(3,2,1)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{y}(1,1,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(1,2,3)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot y(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot x(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.  \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(3,2,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2\\cdot (1-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. If we substitute $(1,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot (2-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,2) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(1,2,3)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,3) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III  only'."} :step/id #uuid "30fe1f41-c7f8-5010-a260-ed3ef63a4abc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be072909-07f4-5637-903f-a70436d30a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449508} {:answer/id #uuid "049c935c-a4ee-5a02-b019-9fd190c4913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509} :answer-field/id #uuid "548fb633-f107-5482-81d5-3893faff9da9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6299e699-f9eb-5bb0-bad6-16b58253ff62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} {:answer/id #uuid "1a54ebb4-bb0e-5d8c-89d3-811768de2241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cc1d60-0069-586b-b1d2-3edcc2ab8a3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3e18ad-85a1-5207-a031-4d811fa7f0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e9a6dd-aa8d-53df-aa34-38929292da67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb845e0-0f31-593e-85f2-f6c94ef89a74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a68d3690-fbcf-53d1-acbf-a5134efed5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132728" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "574ad7f2-698f-50d6-8eee-36f6145d16e5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323e1e26-806d-5103-9c5b-05be999f86f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426704} {:answer/id #uuid "3f71ffa0-c375-5e54-a4b4-82c47e1862fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0<xyz<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426705} {:answer/id #uuid "58a495bb-1535-5c0c-8153-9cddc1a1d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426707} {:answer/id #uuid "66b51739-6d9c-59ee-81d0-4f21e588a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426708} {:answer/id #uuid "ab77e5e6-52f4-5889-9394-0781e3a0ecd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426704} :answer-field/id #uuid "6476017e-359c-522d-8dec-b6d7cd0832c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f23029a5-0003-5b31-a62a-a58e0c29de2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36527" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xyz\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}}$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xyz)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xyz)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xyz| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xyz<1.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d1a2f4bc-c2ad-5ea9-bbc9-a5f92a8d1b61" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26545a18-833f-5099-8272-edbb2a66edaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426703} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5bdc4f-f2f7-5295-b2ba-aa4588793c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426706} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d10897-b8cf-5fad-b81e-91fbcac64c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{y}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426710} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb0a6cd-e867-5b85-88ef-cd845e6904a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426714} {:answer/id #uuid "ff439fa7-e5eb-53d6-8dc0-e5396941eb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426717} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cca11fe-f3bb-53ee-b7b6-de2725b37ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "abe73768-66b7-589e-98db-9176084649e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72006" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{x}{xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d34189a3-bcaf-5744-9e6b-ec97ad8307d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a414987-4111-5550-9b84-35b48e518248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426709} {:answer/id #uuid "c4574d37-c2e2-5922-8956-3143f5bef26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426712} {:answer/id #uuid "c67ec9fa-9f76-59b9-b6ed-a85557705824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426713} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7e5c9b-05ac-5232-9af8-bc55f1d66335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426715} {:answer/id #uuid "f480f4b0-b440-57d3-be41-894b18986911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0f4a07-8c58-5c53-b0aa-71dedf0ab601" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13585dd1-9ab5-5887-870c-2d899c40d4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132652" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{y{sin}^{2}⁡z}\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dd470b32-cbc1-54d2-9ca1-54631aa728a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426723}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426702}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445259} :activity/id #uuid "2432ea49-4693-51cf-9b05-9340c356690d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1625c29f-0f6c-51a9-9abf-da854c690be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0,\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445247} {:answer/id #uuid "603bfd17-5e73-595d-9e4e-67eb66547b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5cf9f7-9ed5-55ea-b3dd-342a00251b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445253} {:answer/id #uuid "b33e9175-1fe3-525c-84d8-993f2fb37971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445255} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f2980b-11d7-556d-9928-b285bb77ebda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253" :question/math-academy-id "q-132654" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b612f2a-e93c-5ad0-972f-c5368a3312ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be072909-07f4-5637-903f-a70436d30a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449508} {:answer/id #uuid "049c935c-a4ee-5a02-b019-9fd190c4913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509} :answer-field/id #uuid "548fb633-f107-5482-81d5-3893faff9da9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6299e699-f9eb-5bb0-bad6-16b58253ff62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} {:answer/id #uuid "1a54ebb4-bb0e-5d8c-89d3-811768de2241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cc1d60-0069-586b-b1d2-3edcc2ab8a3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3e18ad-85a1-5207-a031-4d811fa7f0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e9a6dd-aa8d-53df-aa34-38929292da67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb845e0-0f31-593e-85f2-f6c94ef89a74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a68d3690-fbcf-53d1-acbf-a5134efed5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132728" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "5a19d090-fc30-5d59-972c-c7b1fd1ca305"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e88f5b-98b5-56cf-80ff-82851c3303b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445248} {:answer/id #uuid "395e6ad4-f871-5195-aed0-c82a5612a79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445249} {:answer/id #uuid "410d16c0-a17f-5bb6-8557-36dd6a7c08d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ne 0,z<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445250} {:answer/id #uuid "629c6583-5abe-5cd2-8098-b6cded7ee4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445252} {:answer/id #uuid "a318ea21-95fd-5a51-9e1e-c2501bcf0d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy=0,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445252} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb805034-5a71-5881-8d6e-dc2faac4f6f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07b7a4d5-0d88-5759-b677-08d7e8ac3fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72007" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is defined only when $xy>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5c9fcb15-a332-5a35-8e19-9f260bf4cc92" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445261}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fc5f850-64cd-5893-8891-369f969391e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449502} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7c52c9-c826-5b11-9b7a-993392ba4ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449503} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0737846-2cd6-5da0-9650-75bc9876d5a8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450705} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b64dfe9-6cec-50a9-80d9-4a4526e80263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449504} {:answer/id #uuid "6f04e2aa-8d8b-5312-ad91-326d2690aab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449504} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de809ae-338b-5353-b9c7-b997f94a0161" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd0b63c-f4f5-5c2b-bd85-fa00f3b0bca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449506} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6ab58d-d0b1-5f84-9b1f-39c31befd21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449507} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8beae8-a1f2-5630-bd94-336847d03d63" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9dc50a8a-45d6-510c-8209-df3404b44621" :question/math-academy-id "q-132680" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "7b68aec1-f3e3-5aa1-bec7-f5fcbb362752" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445258}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445261 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d1ed56-bfc6-5bda-a5c6-d5385ab5bd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453940} {:answer/id #uuid "bf41f840-b49c-5384-a274-dfe536fcb33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453941} :answer-field/id #uuid "edafd0ac-42e0-5d4a-be7b-7aa902ffa1b6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454720} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060c118c-98d6-5841-94fb-d05d803c937b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453942} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca503bc-9f0b-57b9-9107-116d82172e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453942} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a453da-0bfb-5722-95ac-96c9ea20e34e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8b8bc4-d09b-5051-b032-eb64e490a6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453944} {:answer/id #uuid "5922a222-d689-5f69-94af-6d12b904bec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453944} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e81b99c-60a0-5dd3-8161-a53cd384fae9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ff98942c-b72f-558c-86fd-786f6575a16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72019" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{z}(0,1,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,2)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-2}=\\sqrt{-1},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the number under the radical is negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."} :step/id #uuid "c05be20d-2e9f-5518-9fbb-273892668ed2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445257}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445246}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494068} :activity/id #uuid "345ef93a-3614-5fac-b24e-50dfc8791798" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e430633-0c79-5bd0-864a-cc54142526aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449496} {:answer/id #uuid "99da71ad-b733-50e9-9bc1-232efc6cef4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449497} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad351a32-8420-5f40-bd4e-3a76a453aa9b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1d2654-71e6-5045-abb7-876f75070a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449498} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1659ae-9585-5275-84c0-eb9540469367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449498} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83c3b78-80d6-5799-ba6a-86046c4eb7a9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b65c5da8-b876-59b5-b16a-4a0a833c5303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449500} {:answer/id #uuid "c93dd213-5575-5cca-b228-64873eafdfe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449500} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1f014c-1d1e-5df1-9579-1cb65d8ea1ac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "72f1f14f-947a-5dd6-902d-418c94ea31fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-132675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "60152d46-a564-5e18-b836-e8be243798bf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a414987-4111-5550-9b84-35b48e518248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426709} {:answer/id #uuid "c4574d37-c2e2-5922-8956-3143f5bef26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426712} {:answer/id #uuid "c67ec9fa-9f76-59b9-b6ed-a85557705824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426713} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7e5c9b-05ac-5232-9af8-bc55f1d66335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426715} {:answer/id #uuid "f480f4b0-b440-57d3-be41-894b18986911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0f4a07-8c58-5c53-b0aa-71dedf0ab601" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13585dd1-9ab5-5887-870c-2d899c40d4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132652" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{y{sin}^{2}⁡z}\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "79e002bf-37b0-5c5a-a563-f450eae54650" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084ca7fb-683b-515d-bd79-6b47f18630b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493404} {:answer/id #uuid "1352f5a0-9197-5682-a147-c4c731774502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493425} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0ef88e-c7ac-5cdc-ae0e-1efc196b01b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493549} {:answer/id #uuid "82a72c3b-2c62-5d09-a02a-39b20b9aa673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493599} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bc4946-5ae0-59ab-8397-c509f69337df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493549} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c27c81b-25fa-5185-a05b-37e0f78454cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "970e7795-a408-5210-80aa-25f375509b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65263" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(-1,1,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{yz{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{xz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{xy}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1)(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(-1,1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(1)(1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{-1}},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}}}{2\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I  only'."} :step/id #uuid "7f1dcee5-fe50-54b8-9541-d95743760a08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9af209-8790-5051-b760-8a2b7ab58c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493443} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae2e06a-4a60-5d0a-89f7-a1206ad8886c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493462} {:answer/id #uuid "4da31340-f280-5ea2-b625-950af86420b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493517} {:answer/id #uuid "ee12a67f-64f9-5a94-956a-3d8cefbc2906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0,\\,z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493770} {:answer/id #uuid "f1099e4d-5739-5e2b-abdc-97a47dbd0d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493778}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493462} :answer-field/id #uuid "544bcd11-f607-53b0-99d1-1273ed387e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f03ff15b-864a-54f0-95c4-85eaad4622ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-132650" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂z}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87fc833d-38e2-5311-a2c8-c3e5fcb40ab1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494017}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b903ce0b-98c6-57d8-9e48-735e14bb633f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448584} {:answer/id #uuid "e89b1128-0cdb-5f83-9979-822275c3412d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448585} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e75cd6-e230-5ef5-921b-7e8382a039af" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b15a164-a197-53c5-b168-a0a97d0c6836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448586} {:answer/id #uuid "c7106db2-aab9-5e51-b215-01c8b91bedc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448586} :answer-field/id #uuid "028cafa1-ca77-5fce-9098-ff08007d68ae" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0b2a1b1-098a-5a44-83b4-193b393fe615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448588} {:answer/id #uuid "5b241cba-015b-566d-8a54-d752edb0b3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448588} :answer-field/id #uuid "17e7993b-564d-5df7-9cb5-369c37279a0f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cee8f940-b17d-518d-bf59-666639ac6929" :question/math-academy-id "q-36504" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y})?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $f$ is defined only when\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}>0.\n$$\n\nAt points in the domain of $f,$ we can compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{y}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x(x+y)} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined at $(1,1),$ since\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1+1})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}),\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are defined there:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1(1+1)}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =-\\frac{1}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. At $(0,1),$\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}=\\frac{0}{0+1}=0,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Therefore, ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is not defined.\n\n- Statement III is false for the same reason. Since $f$ is not defined at $(0,1),$ the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is not defined. The simplified expression $-\\frac{1}{x+y}$ cannot be used to define the partial derivative at a point outside the domain of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f230ec9a-d682-55d8-adbb-af178ec09394" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494025}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493371}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390} :activity/id #uuid "6257a384-69ce-502a-8a3f-5b199a93b00a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125780 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/7e/7ef5d72a30dd7f79f6182c4d78d705a7771f9f90ff0bb50f6d539614cb1e5b4d.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "555ca00c-d37e-5c67-a658-69b087390920" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3522 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4620379b-2eed-5dd7-bf3a-9edf0b4aea79" :step/math-academy-id 19988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :question/id #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" :question/math-academy-id "e-5424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519985} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529139}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "77230797-e80e-5644-8b1e-9e3722a0b66d" :step/math-academy-id 19991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433 :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529191}] :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7ebf299f-89ad-55ae-8fe6-d0af3bc56dfd" :step/math-academy-id 37508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124434 :question/id #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" :question/math-academy-id "e-9962"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446379}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "debf1d2f-c0e6-5c61-b195-a95710e2c35e" :step/math-academy-id 37509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435679} :activity/id #uuid "26e5acff-8db9-5e5d-9122-36baf139baac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b28520-430a-5247-a200-fd5f414e5ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} {:answer/id #uuid "f8033251-ab93-5bb9-a145-e5e99be3d388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3c91e0-c158-561e-ab32-6e72b9ba78b5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "647ee441-bfd6-5c35-84c4-0fc1e7479328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e66778-9876-5718-a6e2-1e95fc527bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} :answer-field/id #uuid "030534f1-c9cc-5372-9ff2-bdc749c441e3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d47ce5-4beb-5f68-bae7-0d361ea95c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454016} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dcdf0f-841a-5b0d-aff9-db735d828b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017} :answer-field/id #uuid "eced16f4-9e93-50d2-bf88-cdda3960f7ec" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea4581af-a083-51da-803f-0f83b3ed2ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79349" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not invertible\n\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\n- **III.** $A$ has $5$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=5$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "42d8e8b3-7048-5f94-884b-ae373fb59042" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f87f060-5b8c-5b4a-806f-8cf93ee4d70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449634} {:answer/id #uuid "4360a160-1403-523e-8e62-4fbb2ec722d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449635} :answer-field/id #uuid "359ebe96-94aa-58ea-b60b-be863ac84a52" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb552ac2-1033-5cf0-95e3-c64844f7abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449636} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cf0040-9b44-519e-ab54-532b2b101a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449637} :answer-field/id #uuid "65159a93-fba0-5a45-abcc-86fe85e1adb6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e5820e-dedb-509b-bef8-5b24f4aea7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449638} {:answer/id #uuid "319a4d81-4806-58b2-9a6b-94494e8c5b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449638} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8aa8650-12f8-56c0-ac84-85ed73892db0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8dad0d98-37c5-5af9-9c0a-4f0fabec2bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141200" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is invertible\n\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9a467517-2fb7-54c3-b455-e47e7392ef56"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235a52da-656a-5cf2-a401-0f42a3642391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459823} {:answer/id #uuid "04790f9e-17da-5ed1-82d4-a7391bccbd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459823} :answer-field/id #uuid "419ff1b3-8e47-5988-b4a7-49adadda7718" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39055a2-7be3-5968-8135-398e60211c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459825} {:answer/id #uuid "f28e5027-719a-5804-a84a-81f68d441b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459826} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ef0960-7c5f-543c-a81b-bf7733be9b0f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3932a553-bb8b-5851-b93b-ecabd828b7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459827} {:answer/id #uuid "909e13f6-b658-585b-af87-0fb072eb5e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459827} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c740ae4-9a04-5de5-a467-c4e121394796" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a5b63a26-4713-5ecd-9d64-befaccc0bd77" :question/math-academy-id "q-34834" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=5$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d2193342-5bc1-5a6f-99d1-42bf1fad6047" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435677}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435669}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485604} :activity/id #uuid "7e6f49d0-b97d-5a05-9f9c-64559727441e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d5ed8d-7e60-5db5-b7d1-d96bb65185a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474362} {:answer/id #uuid "10b14067-3f7f-5868-8791-43396e0607b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474363} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb207fd4-1ade-5c72-8528-ba143a738870" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0948d764-f192-5b49-80f0-17ce4b9bcefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474364} {:answer/id #uuid "f2aade65-5d3a-520b-86ea-745a85070e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474364} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b6ddd3-8ce7-5c7f-874e-23d5563021dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dc218b-818c-594c-978b-8b69736909ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474366} {:answer/id #uuid "4999264b-817a-578e-85f5-209e47509eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474367} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ecf0bb-4395-5d51-99bd-1c0a67d30176" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "251cfe7e-474d-59b2-8ccf-101401daf068" :question/math-academy-id "q-139978" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero\n\nIII. $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $det(A)=0,$ the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero.* $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn,  means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis so the dimension must be greater than zero.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\ne 5,$ which means that $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1140fa5b-f47a-5b21-a507-87491673347d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06680c4f-a621-5dc3-875e-3896db2af04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460023} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1cf54f-05d3-5550-8f3d-cdfa3137d5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460023} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3156979-45c5-5d24-80c1-f665fce48ba8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c8b9c9-048e-580b-90b5-0615fcacbfe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460025} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd76941-0308-57d6-9b7b-3ba6c48171e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460026} :answer-field/id #uuid "44325e4a-3b71-5949-9822-a4ed24085c3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3989b63d-47f8-5757-bb11-6a685f029eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460027} {:answer/id #uuid "16bc9a89-2517-5c10-b196-866970cad27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460027} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9665add-7abd-5377-b1f0-9d3eafc436be" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f4e1182-d195-5cca-bb1a-601ea1f9c921" :question/math-academy-id "q-47599" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}$\n- **III.** $A$ has $5$ pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is an $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $7$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1b87589b-dcb5-58d7-a287-28c231b8ee26" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f8d9af-560e-5e04-801f-49c3569ea09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464240} {:answer/id #uuid "3683cdbb-cea0-5d3a-88ae-46bbf35523a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464240} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8b8cb5-e75e-518a-91a0-160dae2c2bc4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bba33a3-e841-5573-8abf-90651ed20a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464242} {:answer/id #uuid "0c34db9f-4df3-5319-9c26-896a5b27e435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464242} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d917c1-327f-5ee0-8aed-ef99e81a107b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de2f17c6-86e7-560e-b3af-0ab0f7aab9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464244} {:answer/id #uuid "06de2a3a-d315-52ff-898b-03d434ea28ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464245} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bcd47e5-e550-5069-9017-2362f6f7a623" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0c320bbb-0961-56b0-99e4-beaa75aa8f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-142358" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n- **I.** $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- **II.** $A$ is  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2fdad5e3-18b8-51dd-aadf-07ba0badc4d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b28520-430a-5247-a200-fd5f414e5ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} {:answer/id #uuid "f8033251-ab93-5bb9-a145-e5e99be3d388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3c91e0-c158-561e-ab32-6e72b9ba78b5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "647ee441-bfd6-5c35-84c4-0fc1e7479328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e66778-9876-5718-a6e2-1e95fc527bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} :answer-field/id #uuid "030534f1-c9cc-5372-9ff2-bdc749c441e3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d47ce5-4beb-5f68-bae7-0d361ea95c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454016} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dcdf0f-841a-5b0d-aff9-db735d828b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017} :answer-field/id #uuid "eced16f4-9e93-50d2-bf88-cdda3960f7ec" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea4581af-a083-51da-803f-0f83b3ed2ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79349" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not invertible\n\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\n- **III.** $A$ has $5$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=5$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a6dcb993-e91b-5b36-8076-9c0539317c39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e3d0a6-c269-5dde-bafd-a91861927c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426036} {:answer/id #uuid "1848f756-51a0-5639-8084-5aa59abdaebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426037} {:answer/id #uuid "562361ed-d031-5fb2-9175-4571a3994648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426047} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7285e7-4a5d-5073-aa0f-ffd3853ddc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "lI only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426054} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d58843-37c3-5988-8dac-72db97dcd1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426064} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec9750c-eaeb-571d-b465-6fa1ab05e88d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fbccd10-1c43-51a4-ad8b-36063e11427d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$  is  invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b314981c-1803-59e7-b442-57ac10b70607"}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484809}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529204} :activity/id #uuid "266f8ba6-aa38-58ed-8d33-3aec933a1e12" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d248bbb6-50b4-5d61-a95e-58f318131503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464246} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5d0dfe-318f-5100-ac82-83455377b98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464247} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d37791a-a285-5a2f-af41-bcd2d8afc075" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3957e353-c39b-5011-af11-d2c9b6787af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464248} {:answer/id #uuid "8a540690-cbd6-5a7b-8e6a-735ce1ef7a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464248} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfe4de2-b1c4-55bb-a60d-27c7be7257dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f6788d6-5506-57b7-9972-0d793d0b67f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464250} {:answer/id #uuid "67197e1a-6b46-5a73-9b9e-21d0f7711026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464251} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d8c4a3-f095-50cd-bcf7-92ff6b429b75" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142475" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $A$ is invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A)<n$\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, according to the invertible matrix theorem, \n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)<n.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$*  $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0a7d2e71-2df5-5616-a66c-1039ae6b7480"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0bd5dd-c5f7-5e5d-b21c-bcdb4e2b830b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528560} {:answer/id #uuid "7385e402-bf26-5641-9a0b-660e87ee9d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528567} {:answer/id #uuid "e0746457-062b-51a1-b39b-1801dcc9dd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528849} {:answer/id #uuid "e15f2509-6136-5ef5-b754-59b129df8ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528852} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fb30e2-e856-5197-b6a2-1b2e5bd62f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528849} :answer-field/id #uuid "255a02e5-2e2c-5989-905e-69f5223274a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74f4be02-4fe9-5741-a4d1-7268f77a2f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142472" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nIII. $A$ has $4$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2a21ddf3-d51f-5117-8439-7fd92a4b2b2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d885553-3afc-5696-a382-4c860965f60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528520} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf00a72-f79f-582d-9aaa-38874ff20b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528592} {:answer/id #uuid "7df12baa-7b1b-58dc-8f63-3871724e61dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528598} {:answer/id #uuid "c974d506-c2bc-5e7b-97a4-c51ae5371031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528794} {:answer/id #uuid "e94c076f-c6c7-517d-8c55-d74158a4a6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528520} :answer-field/id #uuid "20580d7d-28aa-5c7c-a048-784d0bfa2d4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1d7f6a4-6114-5671-a52c-7df48bee5e24" :question/math-academy-id "q-141342" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nII. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately. \n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn, means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we must have that $A$ is **not** invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.* $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n},$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "94778ac8-3dd7-5564-88c5-443046b2e6fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0a6001-3780-5910-af1b-80f1dc86bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449628} {:answer/id #uuid "711f6d61-9af9-514f-ab7b-3f6b87059a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449628} :answer-field/id #uuid "577301d3-bda4-5711-b8ea-c8d7c59e3764" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd05ec0-7412-5317-bd1f-6fdd48ffae9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449630} {:answer/id #uuid "7e775f74-22c8-5e41-92ca-9b7679c5d763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449631} :answer-field/id #uuid "11747fa2-61fa-56a5-ab66-ef191899dff3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f12245-1e0f-5cf0-8c20-1a321c65854b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449632} {:answer/id #uuid "2e93e8a1-abd3-5bae-8c25-a6156dc77f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449633} :answer-field/id #uuid "71691d56-9200-5496-becc-0f211d28a39b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f63719f4-7a1e-5b43-8302-7786de4d0619" :question/math-academy-id "q-141199" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- **II.** $A$ is not invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "cb8b82a3-c3e9-567b-8dbd-19ce7dce93de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d1fafe-67a4-5304-b42b-0fa34a95eaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467578} {:answer/id #uuid "c76c9ea4-d5d0-5c09-adc5-db52b1568fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467579} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df948ff-c1ab-538f-b99e-ed07682677fc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6428bf6c-1cd8-523e-89a2-bbd6aefca97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467580} {:answer/id #uuid "49e7f113-867c-5ee0-a738-ba5b88651827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467580} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8c77e6-a0b4-526b-bf8e-a435b7b66ac1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b22f319-2182-5f2f-984d-2c69a38c95bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467582} {:answer/id #uuid "f8184f79-583b-5721-92be-e461d3dc1a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467582} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58f3fca-628f-5dd4-9837-348f91ad595e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c600e13-fdff-5526-82d0-6659ac10dfc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-47587" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times $\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e46ea0a8-4991-5f8c-a308-9bc68c5b0d80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529203}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528296}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162916} :activity/id #uuid "402c5791-a328-536f-b52e-96d65b27ede7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049815 :topic/id #uuid "15e1a0d1-d9d9-5488-943d-228c2ac29ecd" :topic/math-academy-id 3062} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163250 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are *independent* random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X+Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X]+ \\textrm{Var}[Y] ,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X-Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y]  .\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** When computing the variance of a *difference* of independent random variables, we *add* the variances!" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c595d74-9638-52f2-a4af-79dfe2ded69c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5023 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85c48a68-bb08-5e36-af92-8eb493ace0d1" :step/math-academy-id 31412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162062 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161862 :question/id #uuid "1df04c71-caae-5a7e-83ac-4dd578b7f766" :question/math-academy-id "e-9122"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52596d3a-a7af-55a0-97a8-8a6158b65638" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541084}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "0a0041f4-e6ca-5360-9d1e-8b7a07a7bccd" :step/math-academy-id 33995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162992}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162067 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161867 :question/id #uuid "0c3690cd-f90e-5d82-ba7b-09d52de47b80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0df88138-7309-5a09-9a96-5e26029f0186" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444650}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Combined Variance Given Some Raw Moments"} :step/id #uuid "24790bde-943d-5850-8dad-fcae56637f85" :step/math-academy-id 34081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162068 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161868 :question/id #uuid "70881840-32f1-5bab-947d-00d77f0ff71e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9181"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3dad37-612c-56f1-a571-360fafe336b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427844}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum or Difference of Independent Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d8885fb1-6c97-579e-9795-07aef731fb3e" :step/math-academy-id 34246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163284 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X+Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X]+ \\textrm{Var}[Y]  .\n$$\n\nUsing the property that $\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X],$ we can show that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[aX+bY]} = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]+ b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] ,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, for two independent random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X+3Y] & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\text{Var}[X]+9\\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, we need to show caution when considering a difference. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X-3Y] & =\\text{Var}[4X+(-3)Y] \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+(-3)^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\text{Var}[X]+9\\text{Var}[Y].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMore generally, if the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ are *mutually independent*, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i X_i\\right] = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2\\textrm{Var}[X_i].\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d56df0e7-9547-5cad-8697-f7c9d96ff12a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5514 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "bcaba5d6-1140-5eb4-b9e1-ea74caf408c7" :step/math-academy-id 34354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162981}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sums of Independent Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162196}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444658} :activity/id #uuid "2dccda47-5766-55d3-9dca-841c1a2a389a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049815 :topic/id #uuid "15e1a0d1-d9d9-5488-943d-228c2ac29ecd" :topic/math-academy-id 3062} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61bb06c0-eefd-5dd5-9a60-e0cb63bc7e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444632} {:answer/id #uuid "62e4e3c6-27ff-5891-9e8d-1d5f6c4b8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444633} {:answer/id #uuid "6bca8e49-1df7-5904-85a2-f8689d1b6976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444634} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdf6d31-4ecc-5d71-be94-0e1394489376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444635} {:answer/id #uuid "b313ad8e-b7ae-5e7c-b6c7-5711116d766f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444638} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9075f10-8356-5116-9d8d-9363c1194e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e466b3e6-da95-5e08-8c31-060b08be1979" :question/math-academy-id "q-129524" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=3$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=1.$ Find $\\text{Var}[6X+4Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[6X+4Y]={6}^{2}\\cdot 3+{4}^{2}\\cdot 1=124.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02103dc0-423e-557b-b063-22b229d61178" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbc9a21-53ce-5107-963d-75a23a6b47c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444626} {:answer/id #uuid "1028e093-da26-54ef-aa43-a9f56a4e0493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444627} {:answer/id #uuid "19ab9ab2-922f-5d8d-a24b-0e98ec213cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444628} {:answer/id #uuid "c9397ba9-a60b-592b-a489-b40297be8dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444639} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eb23d5-5f09-5681-8d44-ab8a69f5b550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "507" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444627} :answer-field/id #uuid "d483090b-1213-58fe-9189-e5ba9f5e3d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4592eee9-4d66-541e-b103-0104f487a3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129525" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=100$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=200.$ Find $\\text{Var}[0.5X+0.4Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[0.5X+0.4Y]={0.5}^{2}\\cdot 100+{0.4}^{2}\\cdot 200=57.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "49059d24-c0ba-5351-8b41-92a57ba5b0cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ede9d25-89b7-5e8f-9713-24a9987fd2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444630} {:answer/id #uuid "34df106a-01bf-52c7-8732-4b94b3d12e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444631} {:answer/id #uuid "d395ea19-98e4-51f7-840e-a5ea2d7b8782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444640} {:answer/id #uuid "f52d6a13-0e5e-5a4a-a0e1-bd443eb90f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444644} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e27671-31ae-5207-9120-d8a7d847ed6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e537044c-a663-5a85-97de-b5e333853075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10f2f9d4-7d62-5e91-afc7-8a268909f88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129540" :question/problem "Consider two independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=11$\nand second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=70$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=130.$ Find the variance of  $4X-\\frac{1}{3}Y.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =70-{8}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =130-{11}^{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X-\\frac{1}{3}Y] & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 6+\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a96e1af-ab36-58c9-a806-16a16fb24b77"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5a011e-bc53-5bc4-8f45-39b4c9f6a5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444629} {:answer/id #uuid "97641fd2-bedb-53d0-a380-c9fdb8513014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444636} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd32b7a-4ad9-58cc-91f9-9026f1bd2828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444637} {:answer/id #uuid "d66b4a53-dbb5-5db6-b6ce-d24df3369ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444642} {:answer/id #uuid "ee19091b-a43a-5af6-af43-e22fe399a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444637} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f087f72-105f-5680-aa6c-80acf118c1cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ebe566e-595e-53c5-acba-b32f38c7b929" :question/math-academy-id "q-129526" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=20$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=5.$ Find $\\text{Var}[0.5X-2Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[0.5X-2Y]={(0.5)}^{2}\\cdot 20+{(-2)}^{2}\\cdot 5=25.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e6bf258-9f58-5ea9-aa72-d9183b6042e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da3aff7b-959d-50a7-8381-c2a8dc46959a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158865} {:answer/id #uuid "e134b7b6-82d4-5373-8265-174fe3a4abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158866} {:answer/id #uuid "25535468-614f-5d32-a108-3120ed29894f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158867} {:answer/id #uuid "53529517-7235-5f48-9f2c-62ffe766939e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158868} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a33d0-cb7f-52e9-b12c-edb84cc778dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158866} :answer-field/id #uuid "e734c878-89fd-5f9c-b368-80d47341e5c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50853964-7ea7-5098-a333-b9b8507d9ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-109099" :question/problem "The probability mass function of the independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n Find $\\text{Var}[3X+2Y].$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of $X$ is determined by the formula \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X}$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.3+9\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5.9-{2.3}^{2} \\\\  & =0.61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y}$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot f(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =1.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =3.25-{1.75}^{2} \\\\  & =0.1875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-2Y] & ={3}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}(0.61)+{2}^{2}(0.1875) \\\\  & =6.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ac8e29e0-f93f-5ae4-ae4f-10bb14033e1c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444654}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sums of Independent Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444625}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162744} :activity/id #uuid "2faf287c-6117-590f-9c73-9dc1ca650d9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050266 :topic/id #uuid "3af6b95a-292c-5d96-81be-58bbf073174a" :topic/math-academy-id 3023} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163177 :tutorial/content "**The chi-square distribution** with $k$ **degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi^2(k)$, is the distribution of a random variable that is the sum of the squares of $k$ independent standard normal random variables. \n\nThus, if $Z_1, Z_2, \\ldots, Z_k$ are mutually independent, where $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$, then\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\ldots+Z_k^2\\sim  \\chi^2(k).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $X \\sim \\chi^2(k)$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/6a/6a46942ee5d42c721fb23905e49a67d28c2916eecc234e86734263c599df49fc.svg)\n\nNote that $\\Gamma$ is the gamma function.\n\nThe diagram below shows $f(x)$ for various degrees of freedom $k.$\n\n![](images/ce/cecc3fc82e618f5b5f91e61c4dcab6c1d38b970af20f0ae2baf929b0ce9cdbb3.png)\n\nThe chi-square distribution is widely used in inferential statistics. In particular, it is used in **goodness of fit** tests, where experimental data is compared against a hypothesized theoretical model. You will learn more about the applications of the chi-square distribution in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "8adfa155-2603-5b6f-9206-c73523985df0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10602 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2249706e-9b5b-5da2-bc3a-0a2a5588a700" :step/math-academy-id 217601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161732 :question/id #uuid "531bdbe2-35a5-599f-8009-f65888b6f05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f80bc6ce-5b86-5883-a2bb-dbdc1e5503de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434098}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability for a Chi-Square Random Variable Using the PDF"} :step/id #uuid "352f1f5d-a28b-5167-a9a4-4b4ead2412ae" :step/math-academy-id 217602 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163178 :tutorial/content "Let $X\\sim\\chi^2(3).$ Suppose we wish to know the specific value $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = 0.05.\n$$\n\nHere, $\\alpha = 0.05$ is sometimes called the **significance level**. The required probability is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/34/34551dd57f17cfb0bdd3bebd2d09c907615f3d936e4b5d69083517d17c1f779f.png)\n\nWe often resort to percentage points tables to look up our desired values rather than computing these probabilities by hand (which would be very difficult). \n\nSome percentage points for the $\\chi^2(3)$ distribution are given below. Each cell in the table gives a value $x$ such that $P(X\\geq x) = p$ for a particular value of $p.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,9.348\\,$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,12.838\\,$ |\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 7.815) = 0.05.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 7.815.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fad9ff3a-2abf-529e-b457-a9b2a3922621" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10603 :tutorial/title "Working With a Percentage Points Table"} :step/id #uuid "2f6a8301-e0ac-56f8-b16c-4cfeffffbf6b" :step/math-academy-id 217605 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161733 :question/id #uuid "1c328fe8-ba46-51f0-acb3-db3781a9d859" :question/math-academy-id "e-16429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492920}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"} :step/id #uuid "84f7b979-7b8d-5bfd-8f98-e8e476f25db4" :step/math-academy-id 217606 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161734 :question/id #uuid "42104104-5483-5c90-be3f-dbe7b10cbff3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492908}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "eded01af-a1e8-5d72-b095-7ea6b9995e46" :step/math-academy-id 217609 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161735 :question/id #uuid "ab5d8857-fdb8-5f4f-85cc-ba9d9e480081" :question/math-academy-id "e-16432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a75e6c3-16e3-50ab-bd36-a2c83ba9288a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050116}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492862}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Involving Sums of Squared Standard Normal Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d2184a6e-f98c-5e40-9508-232f506261eb" :step/math-academy-id 217613}] :activity/title "The Chi-Square Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162177}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492933} :activity/id #uuid "f53cd9ea-6553-5efb-a7b8-f3c13f72a44b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050266 :topic/id #uuid "3af6b95a-292c-5d96-81be-58bbf073174a" :topic/math-academy-id 3023} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35116ec5-d52d-513b-98d1-ee2dc4c2b818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.237" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492328} {:answer/id #uuid "5a842dfb-5f31-5980-9368-0282ea1061e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492395} {:answer/id #uuid "72d1ed37-b2d0-5a6f-8edc-f8327c9400ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492445} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6daf69-0ff6-5b40-87e3-4c42a1bf0569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.635" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492594} {:answer/id #uuid "d8770aef-7d05-5470-a900-6fc714024af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fb6871-9764-57d3-a93a-ed370615662e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9a27da7c-7cd5-581c-8018-40a7c4e9ff5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108258" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(6),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.9.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(6),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=6.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>2.204)=0.900.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.204.$"} :step/id #uuid "14676990-70ab-5297-b2c1-9c3014d28e45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b9b65f2-32b1-5306-a674-1a8cce394049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.711" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492344} {:answer/id #uuid "487a0cb4-3b3c-53b1-ad75-19be1635b445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492362} {:answer/id #uuid "760efcf4-e371-5ee5-8095-2afc058abad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492455} {:answer/id #uuid "801fb805-5abf-578a-bed5-8eed93de26a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492473} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fc4ad5-887a-55cb-91ae-8042270e76d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492640} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb2367b-54fc-50aa-8684-3aa2a710859d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1169ae0-dbcc-5afc-b27c-5d1844b9830b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108271" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$ Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.005.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,0.072\\,$ | $\\,0.115\\,$ | $\\,0.216\\,$ | $\\,0.352\\,$ | $\\,0.584\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X>x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.005 \\\\  & =0.995\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=4.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>0.207)=0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.207.$"} :step/id #uuid "8743147f-7556-5e5e-a7d0-2c7a8c3a230d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5731441f-975d-55af-aa2c-666c2201d3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60.346" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492382} :answer-field/id #uuid "346fec95-da88-5e3b-8992-afee68fefadc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c65221c-bb24-5314-9566-2deac5724c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-252041" :question/problem "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{50}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 50.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\le y)=0.85.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $48$ | $\\,73.683\\,$ | $\\,65.171\\,$ | $\\,60.907\\,$ | $\\,58.135\\,$ | $\\,55.993\\,$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,74.919\\,$ | $\\,66.339\\,$ | $\\,62.038\\,$ | $\\,59.241\\,$ | $\\,57.079\\,$ |\n| $50$ | $\\,76.154\\,$ | $\\,67.505\\,$ | $\\,63.167\\,$ | $\\,60.346\\,$ | $\\,58.164\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\le y)=0.85$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{50}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(50).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\ge y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.85 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $48$ | $\\,73.683\\,$ | $\\,65.171\\,$ | $\\,60.907\\,$ | $\\,58.135\\,$ | $\\,55.993\\,$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,74.919\\,$ | $\\,66.339\\,$ | $\\,62.038\\,$ | $\\,59.241\\,$ | $\\,57.079\\,$ |\n| $50$ | $\\,76.154\\,$ | $\\,67.505\\,$ | $\\,63.167\\,$ | $\\,60.346\\,$ | $\\,58.164\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 60.346)=0.15\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 60.346)=0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=60.346.$"} :step/id #uuid "d35e5081-77a0-59a9-80ae-7f654b2e8813"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a003eec-c940-5c52-8afb-17ca2497bd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492492} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6dfc5f-3b5e-5c28-bb1f-1e1d33b68efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.690" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492518} {:answer/id #uuid "a204a67c-2aac-5f78-851c-80a0a1dad1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.646" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492543} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd4c622-93dc-5665-8894-49abfb499799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492649} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ee4dbe-2f70-5ddb-a8de-19cafb15b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.989" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492518} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb6b39f-0033-59e4-bf9a-66aa3b7e8c0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed8bb07d-ed88-5d7c-a376-b5b20e72fc61" :question/math-academy-id "q-108263" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$ Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(7),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.975.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(7),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>1.690)=0.975.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.690.$"} :step/id #uuid "dd8b0eaf-0121-540c-b3dc-4528fcf15e64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b19f2af-663c-568d-918b-fdd147dba0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492342} {:answer/id #uuid "b7445500-61aa-5ddc-98e2-42d724529aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492587} {:answer/id #uuid "c56686cf-7c83-57b3-ba6f-2e96ffe3b304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492612} {:answer/id #uuid "d9282e28-6b69-59ec-8d69-1f20cf128cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.711" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492644} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7e3563-001d-5b37-b49f-11776d7b682b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492342} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f51432e-ecd0-5626-b4b9-dbd209747e43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4dc7492-4019-5d98-a361-736ee5b48968" :question/math-academy-id "q-108259" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.995.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,0.072\\,$ | $\\,0.115\\,$ | $\\,0.216\\,$ | $\\,0.352\\,$ | $\\,0.584\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=4.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>0.207)=0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.207.$"} :step/id #uuid "e4cf7475-8f17-5f68-9d68-3550e7152e3a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492997}}] :activity/title "The Chi-Square Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492221}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030} :activity/id #uuid "f74c4548-dfc9-5c4c-bb83-8b7024f32b7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130375 :tutorial/content "Double integrals have properties that are similar to the properties of definite integrals of single-variable functions:\n\n- **Linearity of Double Integrals.** Suppose that  $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ are continuous functions on a domain $D$ and that $\\alpha, \\beta$ are constants. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\alpha f(x,y) + \\beta g(x,y)\\right] \\ \\textrm d A = \\alpha\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A + \\beta\\iint\\limits_{D} g(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A.\n$$\n\n- **The Double Integral of Unity.** The integral of $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D).\n$$\n\nOther properties are more specific to double integrals. \n\n- **The Multiplicative Property of Double Integrals.** If $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and $g(y)$ is continuous on $[c,d]$ then \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x)g(y) \\ \\textrm d A = \\left(\\int_a^b f(x)  \\ \\textrm d x\\right)\\left( \\int_c^d g(y) \\ \\textrm d y\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6158466e-1222-52a1-88ad-028b5c5832c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3860 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "15730ddd-c579-5f89-bff8-85f42db15b97" :step/math-academy-id 22622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128989 :question/id #uuid "53185b25-c690-500a-9a82-c18b37668e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-6106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec44686a-601b-52b3-81df-e57e6708fb93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445484}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Multiplicative Property"} :step/id #uuid "2526c89c-75e6-5851-84b4-83ae4eaabc04" :step/math-academy-id 22623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130376 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the multiplicative property when the integrand contains only one of the variables. \n\nFor example, suppose that $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$ Then, we have the general result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(f(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{c}^{d}1\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)(d-c) \\\\  & =(d-c){\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nBy the same reasoning, we also have \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}A = (b-a) \\int_c^d g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3acdc936-b0cf-5aca-a57a-00bdc558bb88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3861 :tutorial/title "Applying the Multiplicative Property when the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "12e3fe87-21fe-5661-a2ed-48880eba8e48" :step/math-academy-id 22624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128990 :question/id #uuid "a5567cb9-1c16-56a6-b0e8-759edf693a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6107"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445488}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e720a516-990b-5dfb-b236-aca8fdfa01d1" :step/math-academy-id 22625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128991 :question/id #uuid "0cb8fa8a-5be1-5f53-bfde-5acf325f6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-6108"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8059a197-e9fb-5306-8eed-62c0cea88f33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541090}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Additivity Over a Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "f422ea91-80bb-5373-9f64-444e0646074d" :step/math-academy-id 22627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130397 :tutorial/content "Remember that the definite integral of a single-variable function can be split up over adjacent subintervals. That is to say,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_b^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nIn the same way, the definite integral of a two-variable function can be split up over adjacent sub-domains.\n\nSuppose that a domain $D$ is the union of two adjacent sub-domains $D_1$ and $D_2$ (meaning that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are non-overlapping regions that could share a common border). Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThis property can be generalized if $D$ is split into a finite number $n$ of sub-domains (where $n>2$):\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\cdots + \\iint\\limits_{D_n}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bc4e2a2c-afd3-57e1-ac95-737c568153b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4258 :tutorial/title "Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "5249980b-32b5-5965-880e-a7948ef2b5f3" :step/math-academy-id 25886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129188 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129007 :question/id #uuid "1c227b12-d40b-53a8-b568-b7bd380acbee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7023"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8505beff-75b8-57bf-9312-e0fcf32f44fd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541101}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "bca38320-513b-5017-88bb-f5d1a8c6013e" :step/math-academy-id 25887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130398 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're integrating a single-variable function over a region $R.$ The definite integral of $f(x)$ over $R$ is not affected by whether the region $R$ is open or closed.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x) = 1,$ and define the regions $R = [0,1]$ and $R' = (0,1).$ Integrating $f(x)$ over $R$ and $R'$ gives the same result:\n\n$$\n\\int_R f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{R'} f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{0}^1 \\,\\textrm d x = 1\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the rectangles formed by $f(x),$ namely $[0, 1]\\times [0,1]$ and $(0, 1)\\times (0,1),$ have the same area. \n\nWe have a similar property for double integrals too. We can either include or exclude the boundaries of the integration regions without affecting the result. \n\nFor example, the double integral of $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ over both $R=[0,1] \\times [0,1]$ and over $R'=(0,1) \\times (0,1)$ will give the same result:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large R} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R'} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the *volumes* of the shapes formed by $f(x,y)$ over the regions $R$ and $R'$ are the same.\n\nMoreover, we'd get the same result if we were to integrate $f(x,y)$ over any of the following regions:\n\n$$\n(0,1] \\times [0,1], \\quad [0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad (0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to double-integrals over non-rectangular boundaries, too." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f51d452-a229-5d76-885e-0fe84f787707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4311 :tutorial/title "Open and Closed Regions of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "45710b0f-af68-5a20-b9ce-c5ecc560f4e5" :step/math-academy-id 26202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034}}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129345}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655} :activity/id #uuid "abdc4a65-40f3-528e-9141-f38a1d8b8f37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc8e0ee-42d1-5e33-ab3e-d149ce209193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128574} {:answer/id #uuid "216a895e-32d8-5d87-938d-db65b2d1bddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419645} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5a35ab-9584-5250-9b05-bab514871947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19-20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419646} {:answer/id #uuid "c482d14a-968e-5fba-92dc-75544ab448dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20-19e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} {:answer/id #uuid "c6534224-e681-59ea-874f-2267428a83f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16+20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} :answer-field/id #uuid "5432046c-908f-53bb-aa5e-d0f6af32c211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9bb5733-5b86-5301-9267-6ae190810785" :question/math-academy-id "q-71994" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{x}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(3-2){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{2}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(e-1)[{y}^{3}]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =(e-1)({3}^{3}-{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =19(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A=1-19(e-1)=20-19e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b0d3e0f-96a8-599d-92f9-04874e8f5b73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fb41e7-e3b3-596b-9de2-56afe6dd5f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9ef82d-4456-5d25-ad26-90ed8d7f4c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346295} {:answer/id #uuid "705f8690-2e07-5932-b07c-8089792360e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{T}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346333} {:answer/id #uuid "9666eb71-130a-59fc-8098-2ba78743d703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346666} {:answer/id #uuid "c006f721-3d51-5d16-a6a8-1b8222268571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}(x+{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef450e5-1471-5ba8-8652-38a2e4337160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e01cd29-3b44-5c08-bc75-551f046b44dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80452" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed0be2aca8d43d733e91f3a8feaaca0649957dcbd516a8218b1e7dea136170d8.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ {x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}0\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b57250fe-0a51-501d-8a2f-9f787d4986a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168581f2-f17c-5d44-a38c-f447560aea9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d9732d-dbc5-596f-ab65-73c4ce86f71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419649} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b906bf-abf8-5f7b-aa2d-46eb0fb79934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419650} {:answer/id #uuid "da997437-2a62-59ee-9431-4e9d511ef420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419651} {:answer/id #uuid "f67323c5-5cdb-58d8-a3cb-89a513553c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e876f8-837f-5088-b5c0-91f0a9d62487" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a49df285-b000-551a-958c-a18cbfff682c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80659" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=-3,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=5,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =-2\\cdot \\,\\,\\,{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2\\cdot ({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-3+5) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f337f9e2-418d-5c24-9790-11f1621a546e"}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419643}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496386} :activity/id #uuid "95d8bbec-04bf-5628-a449-ebbd525dd776" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424c1725-f6be-557b-8ba1-de6cd2c48a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382464} {:answer/id #uuid "ad38cb99-0e64-5f96-baca-e7d0549cba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} {:answer/id #uuid "1e089d84-f069-5f2c-83fa-ebc8d830878a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382466} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fc8d95-fed9-543b-84f8-e46cb703d09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382467} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1aadd1-ba01-5e68-a02a-c2ed4e7c1f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86e0dfb-0f1e-5397-8c0c-93b6e0bbf141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "265058e2-39b1-59f9-b955-fd7951910713" :question/math-academy-id "q-81253" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\pi , \\\\ x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-1,1)\\times (0,\\pi )$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,2)\\times (0,\\pi ).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi }+[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0)+(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5d158e0b-364f-57cd-9861-9ba595b098dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bb7e4f-4f89-5de1-95f7-da1922b3e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495583} {:answer/id #uuid "4abebdbc-99ae-50c0-a512-0a23de882c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495682} {:answer/id #uuid "714c7021-6f5d-5cb3-9a9a-f76210471846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495759} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e8c850-fb04-565b-82b5-6a4a220870b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495906} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b29483-e8e2-58c0-b501-11ed5e1de262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495970} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f162e99-d897-57bf-9c7b-c3f9fc5e3dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "426d96cd-8cff-562b-8539-4a443da3b8d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80668" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1, \\\\ 2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,1<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(0,1)\\times (0,1)$ and ${R}_{2}=(0,1)\\times (1,2).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-0){\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+({x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1})\\cdot ({e}^{y}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1\\cdot ({e}^{2}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & =1+{e}^{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6aea6f9b-1c3a-5b35-93ee-7c684d8d6000" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a89715-7a54-5300-819b-fa080b061464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382450} {:answer/id #uuid "56fb164e-39a3-5e17-815d-ec81eaeb5642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382451} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cb3d91-b5e5-5157-8a43-6dd0b3fbf045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382452} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf0027f-33d6-5dec-9265-dd1640066c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382453} {:answer/id #uuid "5f806e22-cdeb-5e59-8750-426e910009d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ffc185-1273-5e29-8ff4-7540cee84426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9713fa5-57af-5823-80a4-91a27fbe3a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-81244" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-3,0]\\times [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(4-0){\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(0-(-3)){\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third integral is the double integral of unity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(R)=3\\cdot 4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A=36-24+12=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7eca13c9-165d-5443-bea1-7c1c3600f648" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29cb87ec-7b62-54a3-9b91-6761bf4ff971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495602} {:answer/id #uuid "32a207e8-72fc-5597-b6e1-52b672dc0edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495627} {:answer/id #uuid "64babd30-e58f-5686-b182-18182e671ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495734} {:answer/id #uuid "79a8bba4-a404-5ef1-853e-20b00d2df235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495772} {:answer/id #uuid "ec45ef3b-c17c-5ae2-8f29-beba53a099ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495772} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cf7a13-0b14-5e86-98cd-0f8088bad90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4896f06f-886c-5ec8-9b07-a29fce8c9f73" :question/math-academy-id "q-130021" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=2,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=7,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}\\frac{1}{3}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}5f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(7+2) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4b78fd8-e01d-5b99-936b-9991b9a39b49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd94ac7-3795-5961-a7c1-57dd5b247105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495685} {:answer/id #uuid "683d5a4b-306a-5d43-8f8c-20fb207ba1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495740} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9d4d09-68ab-514c-8c81-4e473a25d36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}x\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495863} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b9981d-d744-5f2e-820e-320f4ec86a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495876} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b195e5-7146-58b0-bd21-eaaacb148632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495876} :answer-field/id #uuid "89e973d3-c94c-5288-b1d4-40bdaaeee25a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fc1b49a-d775-540a-9853-9d70731cf1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80656" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c6e4deccce395f5bdc8aadef8cd2c712804235c340ccae174f2067211a5a49c.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ {x}^{2}+y, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eae75d8c-b827-5748-96f3-9fc94f7fde49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496356}}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495503}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541220} :activity/id #uuid "d5e426ab-ae67-5398-8c45-a2967a7a35dc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e15614-cf10-5035-a3e9-1e74b5a8b0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S\\cup T}(\\frac{x}{y}+{x}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380896} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b88361-c3e7-5541-acf8-ca9ffbbb62e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S\\cup T}\\frac{x}{y}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380897} {:answer/id #uuid "180eeba2-deb2-5cf1-b167-3418a77c6df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380898} {:answer/